For a one-dimensional wave, the particle-velocity change associated with a stress change is \(\Delta u=\Delta\sigma/(\rho c)\), where \(c\) is the wave speed for the relevant tangent modulus.

The elastic wave speed is \(c_e=\sqrt{E/\rho}\), and the plastic loading wave speed is \(c_p=\sqrt{E_t/\rho}\). Thus the speed needed to raise the stress from zero to the failure stress is
\[
V_{\rm crit}
=\frac{\sigma_y}{\rho c_e}
+\frac{\sigma_f-\sigma_y}{\rho c_p}
=\frac{250\ {\rm MPa}}{\sqrt{\rho E}}
+\frac{150\ {\rm MPa}}{\sqrt{\rho E_t}}
\approx 44.2\ {\rm m/s}.
\]
Impacts above this speed drive the struck end to the failure stress immediately.

FINAL ANSWER: 44.2 m/s
CONFIDENCE: 98

