For a one-dimensional plastic wave, the characteristic speed is \(c=\sqrt{E_t/\rho}\), where \(E_t\) is the tangent modulus. The velocity increment associated with a stress increment is \(dv=d\sigma/(\rho c)\).

Integrating first through the elastic range and then through the hardening range gives
\[
V_{\mathrm{crit}}
=\frac{\sigma_y}{\sqrt{\rho E}}
+\frac{\sigma_f-\sigma_y}{\sqrt{\rho H}}
=\frac{250\times10^6}{\sqrt{7850(200\times10^9)}}
+\frac{150\times10^6}{\sqrt{7850(2\times10^9)}}
\approx 44.2\ \text{m/s}.
\]
At this speed the struck-end stress reaches the failure stress; higher speeds cause immediate failure there.

